+
+### Comparing cultural patterns
+
+How can we compare cultures without reducing them to national stereotypes? Hofstede's framework began with IBM employee surveys in 1967–1973; later research expanded it to six dimensions. @tbl-culture-national-dimensions summarizes the distinctions (Hofstede, 2011).
+
+| Dimension | Cultural emphasis it compares |
+| --- | --- |
+| Power distance | Acceptance of unequal power |
+| Individualism–collectivism | Personal autonomy versus integration into cohesive groups |
+| Uncertainty avoidance | Comfort versus discomfort with ambiguity |
+| Masculinity–femininity | Competition and achievement versus care and quality of life |
+| Long-term orientation | Pragmatic adaptation and future preparation versus normative continuity |
+| Indulgence–restraint | Latitude for gratification versus restraint through social norms |
+: Six dimensions of national cultural values {#tbl-culture-national-dimensions}
+
+These dimensions describe aggregated values, rather than individual personalities or prescriptions for a firm's structure. Uncertainty avoidance concerns ambiguity, not a known probability of loss; masculinity–femininity concerns values, not a person's sex or gender. For the manager, the dimensions suggest questions about who decides and how much ambiguity a proposal can leave unresolved.
 
 
