 : Questions to investigate when norms are tighter or looser {#tbl-culture-tight-loose}
 
 A hospital can require strict medication checks while allowing staff to question how work is organized. The practical issue is which departures create danger and which reveal a better procedure.
+
+Tightness describes the **strength and enforcement of norms**, not their content. A team can be strict about letting junior members speak or strict about preventing them from challenging authority. The same degree of enforcement can sustain very different practices. To understand its decision consequences, identify both the rule and the response to breaking it.
+
+### Culture, creativity, and implementation {#culture-creativity-and-implementation}
+
+Can the same norms support both discovering an unfamiliar idea and making it work reliably? Chua, Huang, and Jin (2019) surveyed 11,662 respondents across 31 Chinese provinces and analyzed about 3.85 million granted patents from 1990–2013. Tighter provinces had more utility-model patents and fewer invention patents per scientist and engineer, after statistical controls. The authors treated these categories as proxies for incremental and more substantive innovation. Patent categories are imperfect measures of novelty and do not capture every form of innovation. Tightness was measured in 2014–2017, after the patent period, so the comparison cannot establish that tighter norms caused the earlier pattern.
+
+The study also found that tighter Chinese provinces tended to be more urbanized and economically developed, and their respondents reported greater happiness. The surrounding conditions matter when interpreting a cultural association. At work, the proposed trade-off is worth testing: greater latitude may help people propose unusual alternatives, while reliable shared procedures may help refine and implement them. A team could permit unconventional proposals while requiring careful evidence before deployment.
+
+Gorodnichenko and Roland (2011) proposed another mechanism: social recognition for individual achievement can add an incentive to innovate, while commitment to collective goals can ease coordination. Their model distinguishes gains from innovation over time from gains in coordinating current production. Country comparisons linked individualism to patents, a composite innovation index, and income per worker. Their instrumental-variable analyses sought a causal interpretation, but depend on assumptions about how their proxies for cultural transmission relate to other determinants of innovation. A country-level relationship does not show what would happen if one firm changed its reward system.
+
+For that firm, specify the behavior before choosing a cultural label. Is the obstacle proposing alternatives, challenging an accepted explanation, testing an idea, or delivering consistently? Evaluate the corresponding outcomes separately. More suggestions are useful only if some become worthwhile improvements; tighter compliance is useful only if the procedure serves the task. [Chapter 41](41-decision-hygiene.qmd#chapter-41-start) develops ways to separate generating possibilities from evaluating them.
