+## Research Lens: does a messy room make people more creative?
+
+Vohs, Redden, and Rahinel (2013) randomly assigned participants to an orderly or disorderly room and asked them to suggest uses for a ping-pong ball. The number of ideas was similar, but ideas from the disorderly room received higher creativity ratings. Random assignment supports a causal comparison of those room conditions in that experiment; national culture was not manipulated.
+
+A later conceptual replication changed the workspace and tasks. Manzi and colleagues (2019) analyzed 100 participants randomly assigned to a tidy or cluttered desk, using alternative-use and standardized creativity measures. They found no significant creativity advantage for disorder. The evidence gives little basis for prescribing messy desks. It also shows why physical order, norm enforcement, and willingness to challenge an idea need separate measures even when everyday language calls all three settings “loose.”
+:::
 
