+3. **Underlying assumptions:** taken-for-granted beliefs about what success means, who matters, whether people can be trusted, and how the organization works.
 
 
 The distinction directs attention to observable events: what leaders ask first, whom they promote, and how they respond when a forecast fails. Those observations help test whether the stated value describes everyday practice.
 
+An assumption is a belief that has become so familiar that people rarely discuss or question it. A team may assume that a competent employee solves problems without help, that seniority signals better judgment, or that reporting a mistake threatens the organization. Asking for help can then feel embarrassing even when the stated value is collaboration. Quiet meetings alone cannot establish which assumption is operating; ask what participants expect speaking up to bring and compare those expectations with what actually happens.
+
+Founders influence these expectations through early decisions about hiring, priorities, and responses to mistakes. Schneider's (1987) **attraction–selection–attrition** framework describes a reinforcing process: people seek organizations they find compatible, organizations choose particular kinds of people, and some who fit poorly leave. Culture can consequently become more consistent through changes in membership, even without changing anyone's values. Such consistency can support coordination while narrowing the perspectives available to question an established practice.
+
+People also learn after joining. Chatman (1991) followed 171 entry-level auditors in eight large U.S. public accounting firms. Closer value fit was associated with greater later satisfaction and longer tenure in survival analyses. Time with mentors and participation in company social events were associated with stronger alignment after a year and with changes in alignment. Formal training showed no significant association with fit in this sample; much of it concerned standardized auditing skills. These observational findings support examining both selection and socialization, but do not establish that mentoring caused alignment or that formal training is generally ineffective.
+
+Early choices can also become embedded in structure. Baron and Hannan (2002) followed nearly 200 Silicon Valley technology start-ups from the mid-1990s. Their **employment blueprints** were founders' organizing models, which could be implicit rather than written plans. In @tbl-culture-employment-blueprints, the middle column lists the hiring emphasis followed by the basis of employee attachment; the final column shows how work is coordinated and controlled.
+
+| Blueprint | Hiring / attachment | Work control |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Star | Exceptional potential; challenging work | Professional autonomy |
+| Engineering | Technical skills; challenging work | Peer norms |
+| Commitment | Cultural fit; belonging | Peer norms |
+| Bureaucracy | Role qualifications; work and development | Formal procedures |
+| Autocracy | Task skills; pay | Direct supervision |
+: Five founding employment blueprints {#tbl-culture-employment-blueprints}
+
+Commitment blueprints were associated with lower failure risk and faster initial public offerings; Star blueprints showed stronger growth in market value after going public. Founding models predicted administrative structures five to ten years later despite leadership changes. These were observational comparisons. Different outcomes favored different models, and an enduring imprint does not establish that preserving it is always best.
+
+This distinction connects culture to strategy and performance. Kotter and Heskett (1992) challenged the idea that a strong culture necessarily produces good results: widely shared practices can coordinate action while making a firm inward-looking and resistant to change. Their company comparisons emphasized responsiveness to customers, employees, and shareholders alongside adaptation. Culture and performance can influence each other, and the comparisons do not isolate culture as a cause. For the opening team, the practical question is whether its shared expectations bring relevant concerns into the decision or keep them out.
+
 Some workplace practices also arrive from outside the firm. DiMaggio and Powell (1983) used **institutional isomorphism** to explain how organizations become more alike through professional expectations, imitation under uncertainty, and pressures from authorities or organizations on which they depend. Imagine a firm adopting a product-review routine: trained engineers might expect it, managers might copy a respected competitor, or a powerful customer might require it. These are different explanations for the same visible practice.
 
 The distinction matters when deciding what to copy. A routine can help an organization appear credible without improving its work. Shared professional networks may encourage similarities across countries, while different legal requirements and sources of authority sustain differences. For the technology teams, ask where a practice came from, whose expectations maintain it, and what evidence shows that it helps. The reach of a professional community can cross national borders even while its members remain accountable to different institutions.
